course: dictionary
lesson: 6561
title: A word from church and history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from a place name. «The Apostles' Creed and the Nicene Creed are short statements of belief said together in church.» Nicene comes from Nicaea, an ancient city, so the clues are the place and the word Creed next to it.
explain: A creed is a short statement of belief. Try the meaning in the sentence: «The Apostles' Creed and the Nicene Creed are short statements of belief said together in church.» It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: When you see a capital letter in the middle of a sentence, ask why. Nicene has a capital N because it is linked to a place name. Read on to find the answer, and check a dictionary if the clues are not enough.
fact: Nicene describes something connected with Nicaea, an ancient city in what is now Turkey.
fact: Christian leaders met at Nicaea in a great council.
fact: The Nicene Creed is a short statement of what Christians believe about God the Father, Jesus Christ and the Holy Spirit.
fact: Many churches say the Nicene Creed together at services, so Christians of many churches can declare the same faith.
fact: The Nicene Creed begins, "We believe in one God."
exercise: «The Nicene Creed is said together in many churches.» What is the Nicene Creed? | A large building with a tall tower | A type of church music | *A short statement of what Christians believe | A town in Turkey where people go on holiday
exercise: Nicene describes something connected with which place? | Rome, the capital of Italy | *Nicaea, an ancient city in what is now Turkey | Jerusalem, a city in the Holy Land | London, the capital of England
exercise: «The Apostles' Creed and the Nicene Creed are short statements of belief said together in church.» Find and copy one word that means a statement of what people believe. | Apostles | *Creed | Church | Together
test: «The Nicene Creed begins, "We believe in one God."» What does the Nicene Creed do? | Tells a funny story about a king | Sells bread at the church door | *Declares the faith that Christians share | Lists the days of the week
test: The Council of Nicaea was a great council of Christian leaders. What were the leaders doing there? | Building a castle | Racing horses | *Meeting to talk about belief | Cooking a feast
exam: Why do Christians of many churches say the Nicene Creed together? | To cook a meal for everyone | To choose a new church leader | *To declare the same faith | To sing a happy song about summer
